---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac CFM vs Horsepower",
    "url": "/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac Filters Explained",
    "url": "/shop-vac-filters-explained/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Shop Vac Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac Losing Suction",
    "url": "/shop-vac-losing-suction/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-cordless-shop-vac/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-20", modified: "2026-10-20" });
const title = "Ridgid Shop Vac Review: Which Ridgid Wet/Dry Vac to Buy";
const description =
  "Every current Ridgid shop vac worth buying, from the 4-gallon WD4070 to the 16-gallon HD1800 and 18V cordless, with warranty fine print and the right filter.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ridgid-hd1400",
    "name": "Ridgid HD1400 14 Gallon 6.0 Peak HP NXT",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "14 Gallon, Corded, 2-1/2 in. Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The HD1400 is the Ridgid most households should buy, and it is also the overall pick in our main shop vac guide. HomeFixated's spec list gives it 165 CFM at 75 dB, a 10.5-amp average draw, a 20-foot cord and an empty weight of 26.6 pounds. It ships with a 2-1/2 inch by 7 foot locking Dual-Flex hose, two extension wands, utility, car and wet nozzles and a VF5000 fine dust filter, which is a better default than the general debris filter on cheaper Ridgids. Home Depot lists it with Ridgid's full lifetime warranty. The 14 gallon drum is the sweet spot: large enough for a garage cleanout or a modest flood, small enough to carry. HomeFixated notes it is labelled HD1400 on the box and HD14000 on the unit, so do not be alarmed by the mismatch.",
    "pros": [
      "165 CFM per HomeFixated's spec list",
      "2-1/2 inch locking hose resists clogs",
      "Ships with the VF5000 fine dust filter",
      "Full lifetime warranty per Home Depot"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No dust brush included, per HomeFixated",
      "Ridgid does not publish a water lift figure",
      "7 ft hose is short for cars"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best balance of size, airflow and filter in Ridgid's range, and the one to buy if you are unsure."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-hd1800",
    "name": "Ridgid HD1800 16 Gallon 6.5 Peak HP NXT with Cart",
    "badge": "Best for Big Cleanups",
    "category": "16 Gallon, Corded, Cart, 2-1/2 in. Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The HD1800 is the top of Ridgid's mainstream corded range: a 16 gallon drum on a rear-wheel cart with a 6.5 peak HP, 12-amp motor. Retail listings include a 2-1/2 inch by 7 foot Tug-A-Long Dual-Flex locking hose, two extension wands, an NXT Max floor tool with wet shoe, car and crevice tools, a muffler or diffuser depending on the kit, and the VF5000 fine dust filter, with a 20-foot cord. Walmart lists a net weight of 28.2 pounds. The cart is the real reason to buy it: a full 16 gallon drum of water is too heavy to drag on casters across a lawn or up a ramp. We could not find a Ridgid-published CFM figure for this exact model; some listings quote around 172 CFM, which matches an older 6.5 peak HP Ridgid rather than a confirmed HD1800 spec.",
    "pros": [
      "16 gallon drum on a rear-wheel cart",
      "VF5000 fine dust filter included",
      "Full accessory kit with floor tool and wet shoe",
      "2-1/2 inch locking hose"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No confirmed Ridgid CFM figure for this model",
      "Bulky to store",
      "Several kit variants confuse pricing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth it for regular water removal or big debris jobs where the cart earns its keep; overkill for a typical garage."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-hd1200",
    "name": "Ridgid HD1200 12 Gallon 5.0 Peak HP NXT",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "12 Gallon, Corded, 2-1/2 in. Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The HD1200 gives you most of the HD1400 for less. Ridgid's page and Home Depot list a 12 gallon polypropylene drum, a 5.0 peak HP motor, a 2-1/2 inch by 7 foot locking hose, an NXT Max floor tool with wet shoe, an NXT Max car nozzle, two extension wands and a general debris filter. Pro Tool Reviews lists it at 144 CFM and 55 inches of water lift, the only published lift figure we found for a current Ridgid. Sources disagree on the cord: Pro Tool Reviews says 15 feet and a Walmart listing says 20. The included general debris filter is fine for sawdust, but budget for a VF5000 if you clean up drywall or ash. For most people choosing between this and the HD1400, the HD1400's better filter decides it.",
    "pros": [
      "144 CFM and 55 in. water lift per Pro Tool Reviews",
      "Same 2-1/2 inch locking hose as larger models",
      "Floor tool with wet shoe included",
      "Lighter and easier to store than 14 or 16 gallon"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "General debris filter only",
      "Cord length disputed between sources",
      "Smaller drum for water jobs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The value pick for a garage or shop; add a VF5000 filter and it covers nearly everything the HD1400 does."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-hd1640",
    "name": "Ridgid HD1640 16 Gallon 5.0 Peak HP NXT",
    "badge": "Biggest Drum for Less",
    "category": "16 Gallon, Corded, 2-1/2 in. Accessories",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The HD1640 is the budget way into a 16 gallon Ridgid. Ridgid lists it with a 5.0 peak HP motor, a 7 foot Tug-A-Long Dual-Flex locking hose, 2-1/2 inch locking accessories, an NXT Max utility nozzle, two extension wands and a VF4000 general debris filter. It skips the cart, floor tool and fine dust filter that the HD1800 includes, which is where most of the price difference goes. Airflow is unclear: Ridgid's page for the discontinued WD1640 predecessor lists 115 CFM and one retailer quotes 132 CFM for the HD1640, but Ridgid does not publish a figure for this model that we could find. Buy it when drum volume matters more than refinement, for example for occasional basement water or bulky yard debris.",
    "pros": [
      "16 gallon capacity at a lower price",
      "2-1/2 inch locking accessories",
      "Takes every VF4000 to VF8000 filter",
      "Full lifetime warranty like other corded Ridgids"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No cart, floor tool or fine dust filter",
      "No confirmed CFM for this exact model",
      "Heavy to move when full of water"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A big drum for occasional big messes; buy the HD1400 instead if you use a vac every week."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-wd5500",
    "name": "Ridgid WD5500 5 Gallon Wall-Mount",
    "badge": "Best Wall-Mount",
    "category": "5 Gallon, Corded, Wall-Mount",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The WD5500 hangs on a garage wall and stays out of the way, which is exactly why people who own one use it more. Ridgid's spec sheet lists 97 CFM from a 5.0 peak HP motor drawing about 9 amps, a 20-foot cord, a 19-pound net weight and a VF4000 cartridge filter. It comes with a 7 foot expandable hose and a 14 foot Tug-A-Long hose that join for 21 feet of total reach, with 1-7/8 inch accessories and built-in accessory storage. That reach makes it the best Ridgid for cleaning a car without moving the vac. The 5 gallon drum and 97 CFM are modest, so it is a car and garage-floor vac rather than a flood or demolition tool. It has no drain or casters, per the spec sheet.",
    "pros": [
      "21 ft of combined hose reach",
      "Wall-mounts to save floor space",
      "VF4000 filter, takes VF5000 to VF8000",
      "Built-in accessory storage"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "97 CFM, the lowest corded figure here",
      "5 gallon drum, no drain",
      "1-7/8 inch accessories clog on bulky debris"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Ridgid for car detailing and quick garage cleanups, mounted where you will actually reach for it."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-wd4070",
    "name": "Ridgid WD4070 4 Gallon 5.0 Peak HP Portable",
    "badge": "Best Portable",
    "category": "4 Gallon, Corded, Portable",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The WD4070 is Ridgid's compact carry vac. Ridgid's page lists 5.0 peak HP, 110 CFM and 176 air watts, with a 20-foot cord, a 1-7/8 inch by 7 foot Tug-A-Long hose and a VF3500 fine dust filter included. Weight is listed at about 17 to 18 pounds depending on the source. The 110 CFM figure is higher than the wall-mount WD5500's, which shows again that peak HP labels do not separate vacs. It is the right size for stairs, vehicles, a small apartment or a trunk-mounted job site cleanup kit. The trade-offs are the small drum, which fills quickly on any real water job, and the compact VF3500 filter, which does not interchange with the larger VF4000 to VF8000 cartridges.",
    "pros": [
      "110 CFM and 176 air watts per Ridgid",
      "Fine dust VF3500 filter included",
      "Light enough to carry one-handed",
      "20 ft cord"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "4 gallon drum fills fast with water",
      "Uses the compact VF3500 filter only",
      "1-7/8 inch hose for small debris"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Ridgid to keep in a closet or truck for small, frequent cleanups."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-hd0919",
    "name": "Ridgid 18V 9 Gallon Cordless NXT (HD0919)",
    "badge": "Best Cordless",
    "category": "9 Gallon, 18V Cordless, Tool Only",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Ridgid's 18V 9 gallon NXT is the biggest drum on a mainstream battery vac. Ridgid lists it as tool only, running on one Ridgid 18V battery and accepting a second for longer runtime, with a locking Dual-Flex hose, three extension wands, wet, utility and car nozzles and a VF4000 general debris filter on a Qwik Lock mount. Home Depot lists the airflow at 82 CFM. A listing for the closely related HD0918 claims up to 29 minutes on a single 9.0Ah battery and roughly double with two. Home Depot's US listing uses the HD0918 number for a similar vac while Ridgid uses HD0919, so check the box. Register it for Ridgid's Lifetime Service Agreement within 90 days, since 18V coverage depends on registration.",
    "pros": [
      "9 gallon drum, largest in its class",
      "Two battery bays for longer runtime",
      "Works with any Ridgid 18V battery",
      "Takes the standard VF4000 to VF8000 filters"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "82 CFM per Home Depot, well below corded models",
      "Batteries sold separately",
      "Model number differs by retailer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it if you already own Ridgid 18V batteries and want a cordless vac with a real drum."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Ridgid Shop Vac Review",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/ridgid-shop-vac-review/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Ridgid shop vac is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners the HD1400 14 gallon NXT is the best Ridgid. HomeFixated lists it at 165 CFM, and it ships with a 2-1/2 inch locking hose and the VF5000 fine dust filter. The HD1200 is the value pick, the HD1800 suits frequent water removal, and the 18V HD0919 suits Ridgid battery owners."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Ridgid shop vac warranty really lifetime?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For corded wet/dry vacs Ridgid offers a full lifetime warranty against defects, but its warranty page says the vacs are designed for 500 to 700 hours of run life and coverage ends at the shorter of that or when the vac becomes unusable for other reasons. Within 90 days, Home Depot can replace it with the receipt."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to register my Ridgid shop vac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For Ridgid's 18V cordless vacs, yes. Ridgid's Lifetime Service Agreement for 18V tools requires registration within 90 days of purchase. For corded vacs, Ridgid's warranty page does not describe a registration requirement, but registering and keeping the receipt costs nothing and makes any later claim easier."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What filter does a Ridgid shop vac use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ridgid vacs of 5 gallons and larger take the VF4000 through VF8000 cartridges: VF4000 general debris, VF5000 fine dust, VF6000 HEPA-material, VF7000 foam for wet pickup and VF8000 pleated HEPA. Compact 3 to 4.5 gallon vacs such as the WD4070 use the VF3500. Check your model number before ordering."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many CFM does a Ridgid shop vac have?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the model and who measured it. Ridgid lists the WD4070 at 110 CFM and the WD5500 at 97, Pro Tool Reviews lists the HD1200 at 144 CFM, HomeFixated lists the HD1400 at 165 CFM and Home Depot lists the 18V 9 gallon at 82. Ridgid does not publish CFM for every model."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Ridgid better than Shop-Vac or Craftsman?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In the 12 to 16 gallon class, Ridgid usually wins on warranty and hose size. Craftsman CMXEVBE listings show a 3-year limited warranty versus Ridgid's lifetime coverage, and Ridgid's NXT models use a 2-1/2 inch hose. Shop-Vac warranties vary by model, and its small vacs are often cheaper than Ridgid's compact units."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a Ridgid shop vac pick up water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, all Ridgid wet/dry vacs handle water. Remove the paper cartridge and fit the VF7000 foam sleeve, or use a VF8000 that works wet or dry, and empty the drum before it fills, since the float shutoff stops suction when it is full. Use the drain port where the model has one, and dry the drum afterward."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "ridgid shop vac",
      "ridgid wet dry vac",
      "ridgid nxt shop vac",
      "ridgid shop vac filter",
      "ridgid shop vac warranty",
      "ridgid 16 gallon shop vac"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Wet/Dry Vacs
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Ridgid Shop Vac Review: <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Which One to Buy</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven current Ridgid wet/dry vacs sorted by job, with the airflow figures Ridgid and reviewers actually publish, what the lifetime warranty really covers, and which filter goes in which drum.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ridgid-hd1400"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#lineup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How the Lineup Works</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#peak-hp" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Peak HP vs Real Airflow</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Lifetime Warranty</a>
          <a href="#filters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Which Filter Fits</a>
          <a href="#vs-others" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Ridgid vs Other Brands</a>
          <a href="#which-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Which Size for Which Job</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Keeping It Running</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ridgid makes the shop vac most people end up with, mostly because Home Depot sells it and the warranty sounds unbeatable. The good news is that the 12 and 14 gallon NXT models deserve the reputation. The less good news is that peak horsepower on the box tells you almost nothing, and the lifetime warranty has a run-life clause most buyers never read.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose These Ridgid Vacs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ridgid's catalog is large and churns often. Model numbers change when a kit gains a filter or a car nozzle, older WD-series vacs are replaced by HD-series NXT versions, and Ridgid's own site keeps pages for obsolete models with a note pointing to the replacement. We started from the models Ridgid and Home Depot currently list, then removed duplicates that differ only by bundled accessories.
          </p>

          <p>
            For each model we looked for three things a buyer can actually compare: drum size, hose diameter and a published airflow or water lift figure. Ridgid publishes CFM for some vacs and not others, so where the number comes from a reviewer or a retailer we say so. Where nobody publishes it, we say that too rather than estimate. We did not run our own tests on these units for this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a broader comparison across every brand, our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">best shop vac</a> guide puts the Ridgid HD1400 against Milwaukee, Vacmaster, DeWalt, Craftsman and Shop-Vac. This page is for people who have already decided on Ridgid, or who are standing in the aisle at Home Depot looking at six orange vacs and wondering which one to put in the cart.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Ridgid's Lineup Is Organized
          </h2>

          <p>
            The NXT label marks Ridgid's current mainstream generation. NXT models use Ridgid's Qwik Lock filter mount, a redesigned scroll that Ridgid says improves power, suction and lift, and locking accessories. Model numbers mostly follow the drum: HD1200 is 12 gallons, HD1400 is 14, HD1640 and HD1800 are both 16. The WD prefix still appears on compact and wall-mount models such as the WD4070 and WD5500.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose diameter is the most useful way to split the range. The 12, 14 and 16 gallon NXT models ship with a 2-1/2 inch hose, which swallows wood chips, leaves and drywall scraps without bridging. The 4 gallon, 5 gallon wall-mount and 18V cordless models use 1-7/8 inch accessories, which concentrate suction and fit more power tool ports but clog sooner on bulky debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ridgid also sells the same vac in several kits. The HD1800, for example, appears at Home Depot as the HD1800, an HD1800A with an OSHA-compatible filter kit and an HD1800E with two extra fine dust filters. The motor and drum are the same; you are choosing accessories. Buy the base kit unless the extras are things you would have bought anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 7 Ridgid Vacs Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are current Ridgid models listed by Ridgid or Home Depot, ordered by how many buyers they suit. Airflow, filter and hose figures come from Ridgid's pages, Home Depot and retailer listings, or named reviews, and we flag every place a figure is missing or disputed. We researched these from published specifications and reviews; we did not run our own tests on these units for this page.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
