        <div id="peak-hp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Peak HP vs Real Airflow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Ridgid box leads with peak horsepower: 5.0, 6.0 or 6.5. That figure is not what the motor delivers while you vacuum. A 12-amp vac on a 120-volt outlet draws about 1,440 watts, and at 746 watts per horsepower that is under 2 horsepower of electrical input before any losses. A 6.5 peak HP rating describes a momentary laboratory peak, not continuous work.
          </p>

          <p>
            The numbers that predict cleaning are airflow in cubic feet per minute and sealed suction, usually quoted as water lift in inches. Airflow moves light debris and dust; water lift pulls heavy material and water up the hose. Our guide to <a href="/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/">shop vac CFM vs horsepower</a> explains how the two interact, and why a vac with high CFM but weak lift struggles with wet sludge.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Peak HP</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Airflow</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Source</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">WD5500 (5 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">97 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ridgid spec sheet</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">WD4070 (4 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ridgid page</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HD1200 (12 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">144 CFM, 55 in. lift</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pro Tool Reviews</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HD1400 (14 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">165 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HomeFixated</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HD0919 (18V, 9 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">82 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home Depot</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read that table with care, because the figures come from different sources measured in different ways. Still, the pattern is clear. The 5.0 peak HP rating appears on vacs from 97 to 144 CFM, so peak HP does not separate them. Drum size and the motor and scroll design inside it do. For the HD1640 and HD1800 we could not find a Ridgid-published CFM figure for those exact models, so they are not in the table.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is to ignore the horsepower race. If two Ridgid vacs are close in price, choose by drum size, hose diameter and the filter included, and look for a published CFM figure where one exists. The 6.5 peak HP HD1800 is a better vac than the 5.0 peak HP HD1640 mostly because of its cart, fine dust filter and accessories, not because of 1.5 extra marketing horsepower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Ridgid's Lifetime Warranty, Read Carefully
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ridgid's corded wet/dry vacs carry what Ridgid calls a full lifetime warranty against defects in material and workmanship. The fine print on Ridgid's warranty page adds a clause most people miss: Ridgid says its vacs are designed for a run life of 500 to 700 hours, and coverage ends at the shorter of that run life or when the vac becomes unusable for reasons other than a defect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Five hundred hours is a lot for a homeowner. Run a vac for an hour every weekend and it takes about ten years to get there. A contractor running one all day on job sites can reach it in a year. So the warranty is genuinely lifetime for most garages and much less so for heavy professional use, which is worth knowing before you choose a Ridgid over a commercial vac.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">HOW A CLAIM WORKS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep the receipt; the first 90 days are the easy part.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ridgid's warranty page says that within 90 days you can return the vac to Home Depot with the receipt for a replacement. After that, claims go to the manufacturer, which inspects the vac and may replace the defective part or the whole unit. Expect to pay for wear items such as filters yourself.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The 18V cordless vacs are a different story. Ridgid's 18V power tools are covered by the Lifetime Service Agreement, which requires registering the tool within 90 days of purchase for free parts and service. Retail listings for Ridgid's 18V vacs mention both lifetime coverage and LSA registration, and the terms vary by product, so register a cordless vac within 90 days regardless.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason for the split is ownership. Ridgid's wet/dry vacs come from Emerson, which owns the Ridgid brand, while Ridgid-branded 18V power tools are made under license by a different company. That is why the vac warranty and the 18V LSA read differently. If you are unsure which applies to your vac, Ridgid's warranty line or the paperwork in the box will tell you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ridgid Filters: Which One Goes Where
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ridgid's filter part numbers are worth learning, because the wrong filter is the most common reason a good vac performs badly. For 5 gallon and larger Ridgid vacs, the VF4000 through VF8000 cartridges share one fit, so you can change filtration level without changing vacs. The compact 3 to 4.5 gallon vacs use the smaller VF3500 instead.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use it for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF4000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General debris pleated paper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sawdust, dirt, everyday dry pickup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF5000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-layer fine dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drywall dust, cold ash, fine powders</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF6000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-layer HEPA-material media</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Allergens; Ridgid says not certified HEPA</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF7000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foam sleeve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet pickup and water-borne debris</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF8000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pleated HEPA, wet or dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dust and spills, 99.97% at 0.3 micron dry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">VF3500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-layer fine dust, compact</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 to 4.5 gallon portable vacs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two details matter. First, Ridgid says the VF6000 is made with HEPA-rated material but does not meet EPA or RRP guidelines for certified HEPA filtration, so do not use it for lead paint work. The VF8000 is Ridgid's true HEPA option, rated 99.97 percent at 0.3 micron for dry particles. Second, Ridgid lists the VF4000 general debris filter for dry pickup only.
          </p>

          <p>
            For water, pull the paper cartridge and fit the VF7000 foam sleeve, or use the VF8000 if you switch between wet and dry and do not want to swap filters. For fine dust, add a collection bag in front of the cartridge so it does not clog in minutes. Our guide to <a href="/shop-vac-filters-explained/">shop vac filters</a> covers bags, cleaning and replacement intervals across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-others" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Ridgid vs Shop-Vac, Craftsman and Milwaukee
          </h2>

          <p>
            Craftsman is the closest like-for-like rival. Its CMXEVBE corded vacs cover the same 5 to 16 gallon range, including a 16 gallon 6.5 peak HP model, and listings for several of them show a 3-year limited warranty. That is much shorter than Ridgid's lifetime coverage. If the two cost the same, the Ridgid warranty and its 2-1/2 inch hose on the 12 to 16 gallon models usually tip it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shop-Vac, the brand that became the generic name, sells a huge range from tiny 1 gallon units to large contractor models, and its warranty terms vary by model. It competes best at the small end, where a compact Shop-Vac is often cheaper than Ridgid's portables. In the 12 to 16 gallon range, compare hose diameter and warranty before price; that is where Ridgid tends to win.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee is a different decision. Its M18 FUEL cordless vacs are excellent if you already own the batteries, and our <a href="/best-cordless-shop-vac/">best cordless shop vac</a> guide lists the 6 gallon model at 87 CFM per Milwaukee, slightly above Ridgid's 18V 9 gallon at 82 CFM per Home Depot. Neither competes with a corded 14 gallon for sustained wet pickup, and Milwaukee costs more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Ridgid does not compete is fine dust extraction for sanding and cutting. A shop vac with a HEPA filter is not the same as a dust extractor with automatic filter cleaning and a power tool outlet. If dust control is the main job, read our comparison of <a href="/shop-vac-vs-dust-extractor/">shop vac vs dust extractor</a> before buying any Ridgid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Ridgid Size for Which Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical garage and workshop, the 12 or 14 gallon NXT is the right size. It is big enough that you are not emptying it every few minutes on a sawdust job, light enough to carry upstairs and it takes the 2-1/2 inch hose. The 16 gallon models make sense only if you regularly pull water or haul large volumes of debris, because a full 16 gallon drum of water weighs well over 100 pounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water cleanup is where the drum matters most. A failed water heater can dump 40 or 50 gallons on a floor; our guide to a <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">leaking water heater</a> covers how to stop the flow first, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/">vacuuming water with a shop vac</a> covers the technique. Once the floor is clear, a dehumidifier finishes the job; see <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">how to choose a dehumidifier</a> for sizing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cars, stairs and small apartments, the 4 gallon WD4070 or the wall-mounted WD5500 makes more sense than a big drum. The wall-mount saves floor space and its 21 feet of combined hose reaches both ends of a car. The 18V 9 gallon is for anyone already on Ridgid batteries who wants a vac they can grab without a cord, and it accepts two packs for longer runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn between sizes, go one smaller than you think. Most people overestimate how often they will fill a 16 gallon drum and underestimate how annoying a big vac is to store and move. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/">what size shop vac you need</a> walks through the trade-offs by job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping a Ridgid Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most complaints about a Ridgid losing power trace back to the filter, not the motor. A fine dust job can blind a pleated cartridge in minutes. Tap it out over a trash can after every dusty job, rinse washable filters and let them dry fully before refitting, and keep a spare so you are not running wet paper. Our <a href="/shop-vac-losing-suction/">shop vac losing suction</a> guide covers the full diagnostic.
          </p>

          <p>
            After wet pickup, empty the drum, rinse it and leave the lid off to dry. Standing water breeds mildew and the smell goes straight into the next dry job. On models with a drain port, use it rather than tipping a full drum, which is how plastic latches and caster mounts get cracked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the hose for splits near the cuffs, where most hoses fail, and replace it rather than taping it, since a leak there costs suction at the nozzle. Ridgid's locking accessories only lock with matching Ridgid parts, so buy replacement hoses that match your vac's diameter and locking style.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, log roughly how much you use the vac if you work it hard. Ridgid's warranty is tied to a 500 to 700 hour designed run life, so a contractor who knows the hours has a clearer idea of when warranty coverage ends and when to budget for a replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Peak Horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 6.5 peak HP label sounds stronger than 5.0, but the published airflow of Ridgid's 5.0 peak HP vacs ranges from 97 to 144 CFM. Choose by drum, hose diameter and included filter, and look for a CFM or water lift figure from Ridgid or a named review before paying more for horsepower.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Keeping the Receipt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ridgid's warranty page says that within 90 days you can return a defective vac to Home Depot with the receipt for a replacement. After that, claims go through the manufacturer's inspection. Photograph the receipt and keep it with the manual. For 18V vacs, also register within 90 days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Water With a Paper Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The VF4000 general debris filter is for dry pickup. Wet pickup soaks and tears paper cartridges and can push mist into the motor. Fit the VF7000 foam sleeve for water, or use the wet or dry VF8000. Empty and dry the drum afterward to stop mildew.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the VF6000 for Lead Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ridgid says the VF6000 uses HEPA-rated material but does not meet EPA or RRP guidelines for certified HEPA filtration. For lead-safe work, use a certified HEPA vacuum, and for general fine dust use the VF8000, which Ridgid rates at 99.97 percent at 0.3 micron dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 16 Gallons by Default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bigger is not better if you never fill it. A 16 gallon drum full of water weighs over 100 pounds and the vac takes more storage space. Unless you remove water often, a 12 or 14 gallon NXT is easier to live with and has the same 2-1/2 inch hose.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

