          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Ridgid shop vac is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners the HD1400 14 gallon NXT is the best Ridgid. HomeFixated lists it at 165 CFM, and it ships with a 2-1/2 inch locking hose and the VF5000 fine dust filter. The HD1200 is the value pick, the HD1800 suits frequent water removal, and the 18V HD0919 suits Ridgid battery owners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Ridgid shop vac warranty really lifetime?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For corded wet/dry vacs Ridgid offers a full lifetime warranty against defects, but its warranty page says the vacs are designed for 500 to 700 hours of run life and coverage ends at the shorter of that or when the vac becomes unusable for other reasons. Within 90 days, Home Depot can replace it with the receipt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to register my Ridgid shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For Ridgid's 18V cordless vacs, yes. Ridgid's Lifetime Service Agreement for 18V tools requires registration within 90 days of purchase. For corded vacs, Ridgid's warranty page does not describe a registration requirement, but registering and keeping the receipt costs nothing and makes any later claim easier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What filter does a Ridgid shop vac use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ridgid vacs of 5 gallons and larger take the VF4000 through VF8000 cartridges: VF4000 general debris, VF5000 fine dust, VF6000 HEPA-material, VF7000 foam for wet pickup and VF8000 pleated HEPA. Compact 3 to 4.5 gallon vacs such as the WD4070 use the VF3500. Check your model number before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many CFM does a Ridgid shop vac have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model and who measured it. Ridgid lists the WD4070 at 110 CFM and the WD5500 at 97, Pro Tool Reviews lists the HD1200 at 144 CFM, HomeFixated lists the HD1400 at 165 CFM and Home Depot lists the 18V 9 gallon at 82. Ridgid does not publish CFM for every model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ridgid better than Shop-Vac or Craftsman?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In the 12 to 16 gallon class, Ridgid usually wins on warranty and hose size. Craftsman CMXEVBE listings show a 3-year limited warranty versus Ridgid's lifetime coverage, and Ridgid's NXT models use a 2-1/2 inch hose. Shop-Vac warranties vary by model, and its small vacs are often cheaper than Ridgid's compact units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a Ridgid shop vac pick up water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, all Ridgid wet/dry vacs handle water. Remove the paper cartridge and fit the VF7000 foam sleeve, or use a VF8000 that works wet or dry, and empty the drum before it fills, since the float shutoff stops suction when it is full. Use the drain port where the model has one, and dry the drum afterward.
              </p>
            </div>

